\paragraph{Execution Simulation and Verification.}
Execution trace generation improves neural code reasoning~\cite{nye2021scratchpad}, with recent work extending to full output prediction~\cite{meta2025cwm,maimon2026selfexecution,liu2024codemind,liu2023codeexecutor,lee2026solidcoder}, failure analysis~\cite{abdollahi2025demystifying}, and execution-free error detection~\cite{li2024redo}. Verification formulations span trajectory-level reward~\cite{shum2025swerm,zhu2026codescaler}, process-level feedback~\cite{li2025codeprm,xu2025patchreasoner}, per-test classification~\cite{ni2023lever,chen2023codet}, learned metrics~\cite{zhuo2024icescore,dong2024codescore,zhou2023codebertscore}, and RL with execution-based rewards~\cite{wang2026verpo,tang2026execverify}. Aletheia~\cite{venkatkrishna2026aletheia} studies RLVR training recipes for code verifiers; we compare verification \emph{formulations} rather than training algorithms. Test-time scaling~\cite{snell2024scaling,li2025sstar,chen2025vgsearch} and self-consistency~\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} demonstrate verification value. None compare formulations at matched capacity.

\paragraph{Process vs.\ Outcome Supervision.}
Lightman et al.~\cite{lightman2023letsverify} and Uesato et al.~\cite{uesato2022solving} show process reward models (PRMs) outperform outcome reward models (ORMs) for mathematical reasoning; Math-Shepherd~\cite{wang2024mathshepherd} and OmegaPRM~\cite{luo2024omegaprm} scale process supervision via automated annotation. DreamPRM-Code~\cite{dreamprm2025code}, PRL-Coder~\cite{ye2025prlcoder}, FunPRM~\cite{zhang2026funprm}, and CodeRL+~\cite{jiang2025coderlplus} apply process-level or execution-aligned supervision to code. In the PRM/ORM framing, our \cls{} provides per-step (per-test) supervision while \traj{} provides outcome-level supervision. TOST confirms per-test and trajectory equivalence at function level ($p = 0.007$); per-test classification matches trajectory-level accuracy with additional diagnostic value. The PRM literature primarily compares supervision granularity given a fixed output format; our study additionally varies the output format itself (\cwm{} generation vs.\ \cls{} classification).

\paragraph{Repair, Mutation Testing, and Task Framing.}
CodeRL~\cite{le2022coderl}, AlphaCode~\cite{li2022alphacode,alphacode2023team}, and Reflexion~\cite{shinn2023reflexion} use execution feedback for iterative improvement. SWE-bench~\cite{jimenez2024swebench,chowdhury2024swebenchverified} provides standard benchmarks; SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent} and Agentless~\cite{xia2024agentless} demonstrate repair pipelines where verification is a persistent bottleneck~\cite{yang2025aprsurvey}. Predictive mutation testing~\cite{tufano2019predictive,kim2022predictive,wang2024llmmutation,jain2023contextual} shares our per-test structure. LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} and QLoRA~\cite{dettmers2024qlora} enable parameter-efficient fine-tuning; DeepSeek-Coder~\cite{guo2024deepseekcoder} preserves pretrained understanding. Task framing~\cite{raffel2020t5,lewis2020bart,rajpurkar2016squad} affects model performance; we extend this lens to code verification, finding complexity moderates the tradeoff.

